CHUNK({"chunk":"3d","module":"m3","topics":[{"id":"m3-solids-intro","title":"Solids: classification and terms","short":"Solids: types","blurb":"Polyhedra and solids of revolution, prisms and pyramids, the parts of a solid, and frustums and truncated solids.","source":"Lecture 4–5, slides 1–6","tabs":[{"title":"Why project solids","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <b>solid</b> has three dimensions: length, breadth and thickness. On a flat sheet, which has only length and breadth, a solid needs <b>two or more orthographic views</b>.</p>\n<p>Projection of solids represents 3-D objects on 2-D drawing planes. It helps to visualise the size and form of objects, communicate sizes and features, prepare manufacturing drawings, and support design, analysis and CAD modelling.</p>\n<div class=\"note why\"><p>One view never shows the true shape and size of a solid. A frustum of a pyramid looks like a trapezium from the front and like two squares from the top; you need both.</p></div>"},{"title":"Classification","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Polyhedra</h4><p>Bounded only by <b>plane (flat) faces</b>; edges where faces meet. When all faces are equal and regular, the polyhedron is <b>regular</b>.</p><p>Tetrahedron (4 triangular faces), cube or hexahedron (6 square faces), prisms, pyramids.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Solids of revolution</h4><p>Generated by <b>rotating a plane figure about a fixed axis</b> through 360°. They have curved surfaces.</p><p>Cylinder (rectangle about one side), cone (right triangle about one side), sphere (semicircle about its diameter).</p></div></div>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Solid</th><th>Definition</th><th>Named by</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Prism</td><td>Two equal and parallel polygonal bases joined by rectangular faces</td><td>Shape of the base: triangular, square, pentagonal, hexagonal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pyramid</td><td>One polygonal base and triangular faces meeting at a point, the apex</td><td>Shape of the base</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A <b>right</b> (regular) solid has its axis perpendicular to its base; an <b>oblique</b> solid has its axis inclined.</p>"},{"title":"Parts of a solid","html":"<div class=\"kv\"><div><span>Axis</span><b>Imaginary centre line through the solid</b></div><div><span>Base</span><b>Bottom surface on which the solid stands</b></div><div><span>Face</span><b>A flat surface of the solid</b></div><div><span>Edge</span><b>Line where two faces meet</b></div><div><span>Generator</span><b>Line on a curved surface, from the base circle to the apex (cone) or to the other base (cylinder)</b></div><div><span>Apex</span><b>Top point of a pyramid or cone</b></div></div>"},{"title":"Frustum and truncated","html":"<div class=\"vs\"><div class=\"side\"><h4>Frustum</h4><p>The part of a pyramid or cone left when the top is cut off by a plane <b>parallel to the base</b>. The top and bottom faces are similar.</p></div><div class=\"side\"><h4>Truncated solid</h4><p>Cut by a plane <b>not parallel</b> to the base; the cut face is not similar to the base.</p></div></div>\n<p>The projection symbol in the title block is a frustum of a cone.</p>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Two main groups of solids?","back":"Polyhedra (flat faces) and solids of revolution (curved surfaces)."},{"front":"How is a solid of revolution made?","back":"By rotating a plane figure through 360° about a fixed axis."},{"front":"Define a prism.","back":"Two equal, parallel polygonal bases joined by rectangular faces."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m3-solids-hp","title":"Solids with the axis perpendicular to the HP","short":"Axis ⊥ HP","blurb":"The simple positions of a solid, and solids standing on their base: top view first, then the front view, with hidden edges.","source":"Lecture 4–5, slides 7–12, 15, 16, 19, 20","tabs":[{"title":"Simple positions","html":"<p class=\"lead\">A solid is in a <b>simple position</b> when it is placed so its views show true shapes directly. The rule: <b>the view on the plane the axis is perpendicular to shows the true shape of the base, and is drawn first.</b></p>\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Axis</th><th>True shape seen in</th><th>Draw first</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Perpendicular to the HP</td><td>Top view</td><td>Top view</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Perpendicular to the VP</td><td>Front view</td><td>Front view</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parallel to both HP and VP</td><td>Side view</td><td>Side view</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul><li>If a face is parallel to a plane, its true shape appears in that view.</li><li>If an axis is parallel to a plane, its true length appears in that view.</li><li>Front and top views become rectangles only when the axis is parallel to the VP.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Cylinder and cone","html":"<figure data-fig=\"m3-solids-cyl-cone\"></figure>\n<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>Draw the projections of (i) a cylinder, base 40 mm diameter, axis 50 mm, and (ii) a cone, base 40 mm diameter, axis 50 mm, both resting on the HP on their bases (Problem 13-3).</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">Top view: a circle of Ø40 for each; mark the centre o</div><div class=\"eq\">Front view: project the circle's ends up to XY; cylinder = rectangle 40 × 50; cone = triangle with apex o′ 50 above XY</div></div></div>"},{"title":"Pyramid and prism","html":"<figure data-fig=\"m3-solids-axis-hp\"></figure>\n<div data-viz=\"solid-views\"></div>\n<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>A hexagonal pyramid, base 30 mm side, axis 60 mm, has its base on the HP and one base edge inclined at 45° to the VP (Problem 13-5).</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">1. Draw XY. Draw a 30 mm line at 45° to XY and construct the regular hexagon on it: the top view</div><div class=\"eq\">2. Mark the centre o and join it to all corners</div><div class=\"eq\">3. Project all corners up to XY; mark the apex o′ 60 mm above XY on the projector through o</div><div class=\"eq\">4. Join o′ to the base corners: visible edges full, edges behind dashed</div></div></div>\n<p>The <b>triangular prism</b> (base 40, axis 50, base on the HP, one rectangular face perpendicular to the VP): the top view is the equilateral triangle with one side perpendicular to XY; the front view is a rectangle 34.6 mm wide (the height of the triangle, 40 × √3/2) and 50 mm high.</p>"},{"title":"Changing the position","html":"<p class=\"lead\">For a solid standing on its base, only the <b>top view's orientation</b> changes from one position to the next; the front view follows from it.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Prism</b> on its base: a rectangular face parallel to the VP, perpendicular to the VP, inclined at φ to the VP, or two faces equally inclined to the VP.</li><li><b>Pyramid</b> on its base: a base edge parallel to the VP, perpendicular to the VP, inclined at φ, or equally inclined.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>A square pyramid, base 40 mm side, axis 60 mm, rests on its base on the HP. Draw its views when (a) a side of the base is parallel to the VP, (b) a side is inclined at 30° to the VP, (c) all sides are equally inclined to the VP (slide 20).</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">Top view: a 40 mm square with its side at 0°, 30° and 45° to XY; diagonals give the slant edges</div><div class=\"eq\">Front view: project the corners up; apex 60 mm above XY</div></div></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Axis perpendicular to the HP: which view is drawn first?","back":"The top view (it shows the true shape of the base)."},{"front":"Axis perpendicular to the VP: which view first?","back":"The front view."},{"front":"Axis parallel to both HP and VP: which view first?","back":"The side view."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m3-solids-vp","title":"Solids with the axis perpendicular to the VP or parallel to both planes","short":"Axis ⊥ VP, ∥ both","blurb":"Prisms, pyramids, cylinders and cones lying with the axis perpendicular to the VP (front view first) or parallel to both planes (side view first).","source":"Lecture 4–5, slides 13, 14, 17, 18, 21–27","tabs":[{"title":"Axis ⊥ VP","html":"<figure data-fig=\"m3-solids-axis-vp\"></figure>\n<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>A pentagonal prism, end edge 20 mm, long edges 50 mm, has its pentagonal ends parallel to the VP and one rectangular face at 45° to the HP towards the left (slide 13).</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">Front view: draw the end edge a′b′ = 20 mm at 45° to XY with b′ on XY, rising to the left; build the regular pentagon on it</div><div class=\"eq\">Top view: project down, keep the ends parallel to XY, length 50 mm</div><div class=\"eq\">The long edge through b is the lowest, so it is hidden from above: dashed</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The slide's solution text says a′b′ = 30 mm, but the problem and its drawing use 20 mm. Use 20.</p></div>"},{"title":"Pyramids ⊥ VP","html":"<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>A square pyramid, base 40 mm side, axis 65 mm, has its base in the VP. One base edge is inclined at 30° to the HP and a corner on that edge is on the HP (Problem 13-8).</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">Front view: draw d′c′ = 40 at 30° to XY with d′ on XY; complete the square a′b′c′d′; join the diagonals to find o′</div><div class=\"eq\">Top view: the base is in the VP, so the base corners lie on XY; the apex o is 65 mm below XY on the projector through o′</div><div class=\"eq\">Join o to the corners; edges hidden from above are dashed</div></div></div>\n<p>Other positions on the slides: a hexagonal pyramid with its base on the rear and two opposite base edges at 45° to the HP (slide 14); pyramids with the axis ⊥ VP having a base edge on the HP, a corner on the HP, or a corner on the HP with two base edges equally inclined (slide 17).</p>"},{"title":"Cylinder and cone ⊥ VP","html":"<ul><li>A <b>cylinder</b> resting on the HP with its axis perpendicular to the VP touches the HP along <b>one generator</b>. Front view: a circle touching XY. Top view: a rectangle.</li><li>A <b>cone</b> resting on the HP with its axis perpendicular to the VP touches the HP at <b>one point of the base circle</b>. Front view: a circle touching XY. Top view: a triangle.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Axis ∥ both planes","html":"<p class=\"lead\">When the axis is parallel to <b>both</b> the HP and the VP, neither the front view nor the top view shows the true shape of the ends. Begin with the <b>side view</b>, then project the front view horizontally and the top view from both.</p>\n<div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"given\"><p>A triangular prism, base 40 mm side, axis 65 mm, rests on the HP on one rectangular face with its axis parallel to the VP (Problem 13-9).</p></div><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">Side view: an equilateral triangle of 40 mm side with one side on XY</div><div class=\"eq\">Front view: project horizontally; a rectangle 65 long, as high as the triangle (34.6 mm), with the axis as a centre line</div><div class=\"eq\">Top view: project down from the front view and across from the side view</div></div></div>\n<p>The same idea solves a horizontal hexagonal prism (25 mm end edges, 60 long) resting on a long edge with two faces perpendicular to the HP, and a pentagonal pyramid (30 mm base, 70 mm axis) with a horizontal axis resting on one base corner (slides 25, 26), using a <b>helping end view</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"note mnemonic\"><p>The view you draw first is the one where the axis is a <b>point</b>: that view shows the true shape.</p></div>"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Pentagonal prism with ends parallel to the VP: which view first?","back":"Front view (the pentagon is true shape)."},{"front":"Why is one long edge of that prism dashed in the top view?","back":"It is the lowest edge, hidden from above by the solid."},{"front":"Square pyramid with its base in the VP: where do the base corners appear in the top view?","back":"On the XY line."}],"cardCount":7,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[{"id":"m3-solids-cyl-cone","src":"m3/pages/l45-08.png","crop":null,"caption":"Cylinder and cone (base Ø40, axis 50) standing on the HP: circle in the top view, rectangle and triangle in the front view.","alt":"Front view rectangle and triangle above XY, two circles below XY."},{"id":"m3-solids-axis-hp","src":"m3/pages/l45-15.png","crop":null,"caption":"Hexagonal pyramid (base 30, axis 60, an edge at 45° to the VP) and triangular prism (base 40, axis 50) on their bases.","alt":"Front view of a hexagonal pyramid with dashed hidden slant edges, its hexagon top view, and a prism rectangle over a triangle."},{"id":"m3-solids-axis-vp","src":"m3/pages/l45-13.png","crop":null,"caption":"Pentagonal prism (ends parallel to the VP, a face at 45° to the HP) and square pyramid (base in the VP, edge at 30° to the HP): front view first.","alt":"Pentagon and tilted square above XY, a rectangle with one dashed line and a triangle below XY."}]});
